## ABSTRACT
The  preparation  of  supported  high-density  metal  nanoparticles  (NPs)  is  of  great  importance  to  boost  the  performance  in heterogeneous catalysis. Thermal transformation of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) has been demonstrated as a promising route for the synthesis of supported metal NPs with high metal loadings, but it is challenge to achieve uniform metal dispersion. Here we report a strategy of “spatial isolation and dopant anchoring” to resist metal aggregation in the pyrolysis of MOFs through converting a bulk MOF into dual-heteroatom-containing flower-like MOF sheets (B/N-MOF-S). This approach can spatially isolate metal ions and increase the number of anchoring sites, thus efficiently building physical and/or chemical barriers to cooperatively prevent metal NPs from aggregation in the high-temperature transformation process. After thermolysis at $1,000^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , the B/N-MOFS affords B,N co-doped carbon-supported Co NPs (Co/BNC) with uniform dispersion and a high Co loading of 37.3 wt. $\%$ , while untreated bulk MOFs yield much larger sizes and uneven distribution of Co NPs. The as-obtained $\mathsf{C o/B N C}$ exhibits  excellent electrocatalytic activities in both hydrogen evolution and hydrazine oxidation reactions, and only a voltage of $0.617\mathrm{~V~}$ at a high current density of $100\mathsf{m A}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ is required when applied to a two-electrode overall hydrazine splitting electrolyzer.
## KEYWORDS
electrocatalysis, metal-organic frameworks, metal nanoparticles, overall hydrazine splitting, thermal transformation
## Introduction
Carbon-supported  metal  nanoparticles  (NPs),  a  major  class  of nanomaterials, are of great importance for a variety of applications including  catalysis.  Their  catalytic  performances  are  determined by the number and intrinsic activity of the active sites [1–8]. To increase  the  number  of  exposed  sites  in  metal  catalysts,  it  is important to increase the metal loadings and improve the metal dispersion.  Most  supported  metal  NPs  are  prepared  by impregnation methods, but it is difficult to obtain well dispersed metal NPs with high density.
Recently, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), with facile tuning of compositions and precise arrangement of elements at molecular and  atomic  levels  [9–11],  have  been  considered  as  promising precursors for the synthesis of carbon-supported metal NPs with high metal loadings [12–17]. However, in most bulk MOFs, the dense  metal  sites  in  a  three-dimensional  lattice  are  prone  to migrate  and  aggregate  during  the  calcination  treatments  [18]. There are two general strategies, i.e., “metal-dopant isolation” and “heteroatom-dopant  anchoring”,  that  have  been  widely  used  to alleviate  the  issue  of  serious  metal  aggregation  in  the  thermal transformation of MOFs. The metal-dopant isolation strategy uses a  second  metal  that  is  easily  removed  to  expand  the  adjacent distances  of  targeted  metals  [19–22].  However,  this  method sacrifices  the  loading  amount  of  metal  on  carbon,  which  would limit their overall catalytic performances. On the other hand, the “heteroatom-dopant anchoring” strategy introduces heteroatoms (e.g., N and S) on ligands to act as anchors for the immobilization of metal NPs [23–29]. However, the low heteroatom content in MOF  ligand  is  not  capable  of  preventing  the  large  number  of metal  species  from  aggregation.  To  address  the  challenge,  the development of effective approaches is needed to address the tradeoff  between  metal  loading  and  dispersion  for  MOF-derived carbon-supported metal NPs.
Here,  we  propose  a  strategy  of “spatial  isolation  and  dopant anchoring” to resist metal aggregation in the pyrolysis of MOFs for  the  synthesis  of  carbon-supported  metal  NPs  with  both uniform dispersion and high loading. As a proof-of-concept, we convert  a  well-known  bulk  zeolitic  imidazolate  framework,  i.e., ZIF- $8(Z{\bmod{\mathrm{~,~}}}\ \mathrm{Co})$ ,  to  flower-like  boron/nitrogen-containing  MOF sheets  (B/N-MOF-S)  using  boronic  acid $\mathrm{(H}_{3}\mathrm{BO}_{3})$ as  an  etching agent.  The  flower-like  structure  can  spatially  isolate  metal  ions within the layers and thus reduce the tendency of metal ions in different  layers  to  contact  with  each  other.  Moreover,  the  dual ligands  of  B/N-MOF-S  (i.e.,  imidazole  and  borate)  with  high contents of N and B heteroatoms can provide strong interaction with  metal  ions.  These  two  effects  can  efficiently  build  physical and/or chemical barriers to cooperatively prevent the aggregation of metal NPs during high-temperature pyrolysis. After calcination at $1{,}000^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the flower-like B/N-MOF-S is converted to Co NPs supported on B,N co-doped carbon $(\mathrm{Co/BNC)}$ with an average $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ size of about $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ , much smaller than those derived from bulk ZIF- $8(Z{\bmod{\mathrm{~Co}}})$ with  similar $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ loading  of  ca. $37\ \mathrm{wt.}\%$ .  The resulting $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ exhibits  excellent  electrocatalytic  activities  in the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and hydrazine oxidation reaction $\left(\mathrm{HzOR}\right)$ .  Moreover,  a  two-electrode  overall  hydrazine splitting electrolyze employed $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ as both cathode and anode materials  only  requires  a  voltage  of $0.617\mathrm{~V~}$ to  achieve  a  high current  density  of $100\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .  Experimental  results  and theoretical calculations reveal that the good dispersion of small Co NPs  with  a  high  number  of  exposed  active  sites  and  electrondeficient state of Co center with high intrinsic activity contribute to the excellent HER and HzOR performances of Co/BNC.
## 2    Experimental section
## 2.1    Chemicals
Zinc  acetate  dihydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ $99\%$ ,  Aladdin  Industrial Corporation), cobalt(II) acetate tetrahydrate $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , $99\%$ , Aladdin  Industrial  Corporation),  2-methylimidazole $\mathrm{(C_{4}H_{6}N_{2}},$ $98\%$ ,  Aladdin  Industrial  Corporation),  boric  acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B O_{3},}$ $99\%$ Tianjin Damao Chemical Reagent Factory), potassium hydroxide (KOH, $99\%$ , Aladdin Industrial Corporation), methanol $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH},$ $\ge~99.7\%$ ,  Guangdong  Guanghua  Sci-Tech $\mathrm{Co}$ .,  Ltd.), $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $(20\ \mathrm{wt}.\%$ ,  Aladdin  Industrial  Corporation), $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ $20\ \mathrm{wt}.\%$ ,  Alfa Aesar),  Nafion  (5  wt. $\%$ ,  Hesen),  and  hydrazinium  hydrate $(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $50\%$ , Aladdin Industrial Corporation). All chemicals were directly used without further treatment.
## 2.2    Materials preparation
Preparation  of  ZIF- ${\bf8(Z n,\ C o)}$ . First, $\mathbf{1.12\g}$ 2-methylimidazole $\mathrm{(C_{4}H_{6}N_{2})}$ was dissolved in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ water (denoted as solution A). $0.25\mathrm{g}\mathrm{Zn(Ac)}_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $0.05\mathrm{~g~Co(Ac)_{2}{\cdot}4H_{2}O}$ were dissolved in $5\mathrm{mL}$ water (solution B). Subsequently, the solution B was poured into  A.  After  stirring  for $^{10\mathrm{~s,~}}$ the  mixed  solution  was  left undisturbed at room temperature for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Finally, the sample was washed with methanol and dried in an oven at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
Preparation of B/N-MOF-S. First, the as-prepared ZIF- $8(\mathrm{Zn},$ Co) was dispersed in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol solution. $2.4\mathrm{~g~H_{3}B O_{3}}$ was dissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol solution. The above two solutions were  mixed  and  stirred  for $15~\mathrm{\min}$ ,  then  the  mixture  was transferred into a $100~\mathrm{mL}$ stainless steel autoclave and reacted at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize5h}$ .  After  reaction,  the  products  were  collected  by centrifugation and washed three times with methanol and dried in an oven at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ .
Preparation of ZIF- ${\bf8(Z n,C o)/B/N}$ -MOF-S hybrids. The ZIF$8(\mathrm{Zn},\mathrm{Co})/\mathrm{B}/\mathrm{N}{-}\mathrm{MOF}{-}\mathrm{S}$ hybrid product was prepared by the same preparation  procedure  as  B/N-MOF-S,  except  for  heating  at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $3\mathrm{h}$ .
Preparation of $\mathbf{Co}/\mathbf{NC}$ and $\mathbf{Co/BNC}.$ . The dried ZIF- $3(Z{\bmod{\mathrm{~Co}}})$ and B/N-MOF-S samples were then calcined at $1,000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ with  a  heating  rate  of $2.5~\mathrm{^{\circ}C\cdot m i n^{-1}}$ in  argon  atmosphere.  The obtained materials were denoted as $\mathrm{Co/NC}$ and ${\mathrm{Co/BNC}},$ using ZIF- $8(Z{\bmod{\mathrm{~Co}}})$ and B/N-MOF-S as the precursor, respectively.
Preparation of $\mathbf{Co}/\mathbf{NC}/\mathbf{Co}/\mathbf{BNC}.$ The Co/NC/Co/BNC hybrid product  was  prepared  by  the  same  preparation  procedure  as ${\mathrm{Co/BNC}},$ except  for  using  ZIF-8(Zn,  Co)/B/N-MOF-S  as  the precursor.
Preparation  of  D-Co/BNC. $_{0.21\mathrm{~g~}}$ ZIF-8(Zn,  Co)  and $2.4\mathrm{~g~}$ $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BO}_{3}$ were  mixed  and  ground,  and  they  were  calcined  at $1,000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ with  a  heating  rate  of $2.5~\mathrm{^{\circ}C}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ in  argon atmosphere. The obtained material was denoted as D-Co/BNC.
## 3    Results and discussion
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images show the morphology revolution from the rhombic dodecahedral shape of ZIF- $3(Z{\bmod{\mathrm{~Co}}})$ (Figs. 1(b) and 1(c), and Fig. S2 in the ESM) to the flower-like shape of B/N-MOFS assembled by nanosheets (Figs. 1(d) and 1(e), and Fig. S3 in the ESM). The selected area electron diffraction (SAED) patterns of B/N-MOF-S reveal its polycrystalline nature (Fig. S3 in the ESM). The  high  magnification  TEM  image  and  corresponding  fast Fourier  transform  (FFT)  patterns  indicate  the  ordered  crystal structure  of  the  individual  sheet  in  B/N-MOF-S  (Fig. S3  in  the ESM).  High-angle  annular  dark-field  scanning  transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) images, the corresponding energy  dispersive  X-ray  spectroscopy  (EDS)  elemental  mapping, and line scanning demonstrate that Zn, Co, O, N, and C elements are evenly distributed in the B/N-MOF-S (Fig. S4 in the ESM).
The identification of B in B/N-MOF-S by EDS analysis is not successful  because  of  the  overlap  between  peaks  of  B  and  C (Fig. S5  in  the  ESM).  Nevertheless,  Fourier  transform  infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) implies the existence of B in the B–O group with  its  asymmetric  and  symmetric  stretching  vibration  at $1,200{-}1,350\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Fig. $\mathbf{l}(\mathbf{g}))$ [31]. Moreover, the high-resolution B 1s X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra show one peak at $191.8\mathrm{eV}$ , corresponding to the B–O group (Fig. S6 in the ESM). The high-resolution N 1s spectra can be divided into two peaks at 398.5 and $400.0~\mathrm{eV};$ , assigned to N–C and $_{\mathrm{N=C}}$ bonds in the 2- methylimidazole  ligand  (Fig. S7  in  the  ESM),  with  intensity weaker than that of ZIF- $8(Z{\bmod{\mathrm{~Co}}})$ . These results demonstrate that the imidazole ligand was partially substituted by $\mathrm{BO}_{3}{}^{3-}$ , resulting in the  conversion  of  the  dodecahedron-like  ZIF- $8(\mathrm{Zn},\ \mathrm{Co})$ into flower-like B/N-MOF-S sheets with dual ligands.
Nitrogen  sorption  experiments  were  further  performed  to investigate the porosity of the materials. Distinct from the parent ZIF- $8(Z{\bmod{\ }}\ \mathrm{~Co})$ with  dominant  microporous  structures,  the adsorption isotherm of B/N-MOF-S shows a sharp increment in nitrogen uptake at a low relative pressure of 0–0.1 and a hysteresis loop  at  high  pressures,  suggesting  the  presence  of  micro-  and mesopores, which are also confirmed by the corresponding pore size distribution curves (Fig. S8 in the ESM).
Next, ZIF- ${\mathrm{8(Zn,Co)}}$ and B/N-MOF-S were directly carbonized at $1{,}000\ {^{\circ}}\mathrm{C}$ under  a  flow  of  argon  (Ar)  to  obtain  Co  NPs embedded in N-doped carbon (denoted as $\mathsf{C o}/\mathsf{N C})$ and ${\mathrm{Co~NPs}}$ in  B,N  co-doped  carbon  (denoted  as $\mathrm{Co/BNC)}$ ,  respectively. $\mathrm{Co/NC}$ and $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ show  similar  Co  loadings  (37.5  vs. $37.3\mathrm{\wt.\%}$ ),  as  determined  by  AAS  (Table  S1  in  the  ESM).  The
XRD  patterns  of  both $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{NC}$ and $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ display  three diffraction peaks at around $44.1\ensuremath{^\circ}_{:}$ , $51.3^{\circ}{\mathrm{:}}$ , and $75.7\%$ , indexed to the (111), (200), and (220) planes of metallic Co (PDF No. 15-0806), respectively (Fig. 2(i)). Co/BNC exhibits a lower Co intensity than ${\mathrm{Co/NC}},$ indicating the smaller particle size of Co in $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ (Fig. 2(i)). From the SEM, TEM, and HAADF-STEM images, Co/BNC shows a homogeneous distribution of Co NPs with small particle sizes of around $10\mathrm{nm}$ (Figs. 2(e)–2(g), and Figs. S9 and S10 in the ESM). The interplanar spacings of metal NPs and carbon layers were measured to be 0.21 and $0.34\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the (111) plane of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ and the (002) plane of graphitic carbon, respectively (Fig. S11  in  the  ESM).  HAADF-STEM  images  and  the corresponding EDS elemental mapping confirm that Co NPs are uniformly  dispersed  in  the  carbon  supports  composed  of  C,  N, and O elements (Fig. $2\mathrm{(h)}$ and Fig. S12 in the ESM). In addition, the electron energy loss spectroscopy (EELS) further verifies the existence  of  B  elements  in  the  carbon  support  of $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ (Fig. 2(j)).  For  comparison, ${\mathrm{Co/NC}}$ exhibits  uneven  distribution  and serious aggregation of Co NPs (Figs. 2(a)–2(d), and Figs. S13 and S14 in the ESM).
Raman  spectra  exhibit $\mathrm{~D~}$ band  and  G  band  at  1,350  and $1{,}590\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , corresponding to the disordered and graphitic carbon, respectively. $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ has a higher $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ ratio than $\mathrm{Co/NC}$ (1.09 vs.  1.01)  (Fig. S15  in  the  ESM),  implying  that $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ has  a higher  level  of  heteroatom  doping,  which  is  consistent  with  the elemental  analysis  results  (Table  S1  in  the  ESM).  The $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption  isotherms  of $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ and $\mathrm{Co/NC}$ both show a typical curve of type IV, revealing the presence of microand mesopores (Fig. S16 in the ESM). The formation of abundant mesopores in the derived carbon composites may result from the evaporation of Zn during the pyrolysis.
The XPS survey spectrum of Co/BNC indicates the existence of
Co, C, N, B, and O (Fig. S17(a) in the ESM). In the B 1s spectra, three  boron-containing  species  including  B–C  ( $191.3\ \mathrm{eV},$ ),  B–N $(192.6\mathrm{eV})$ , and B–O (193.4 eV) can be detected in Co/BNC (Fig. S17(b) in the ESM) [32]. Furthermore, the N 1s spectra may be deconvoluted  into  five  peaks  at  398.3,  399.1,  400.2,  401.6,  and $403.5\ \mathrm{eV},$ assigned to N–B, pyridinic–N, Co–N, pyrrolic–N, and graphite–N, respectively (Fig. S17(d) in the ESM) [33]. The C 1s spectra reveal the presence of C–O, C–N, C–C, and C–B bonds at 288.7, 285.9, 284.9, and $284.5~\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively (Fig. $2(\mathrm{k}))$ [34]. Xray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectra of $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ also confirm the successful doping of B and N heteroatoms in the carbon matrix (Fig. S18 in the ESM). For ${\mathrm{Co/NC}},$ the XPS spectra demonstrate the single doping of N in the carbon network (Fig. S17 in the ESM).
The  above  characterization  results  suggest  the  obtained  small and even Co particles on $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ might be related to the flowerlike  morphology  and/or  the  B  doping.  We  further  performed controlled  experiments  to  clarify  the  role  of  morphology  and doping level in preventing the Co NPs against aggregation during pyrolysis. ZIF- $8(\mathrm{Zn},\mathrm{Co})$ directly mixed with $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BO}_{3}$ powders was subjected to carbonization at $1{,}000^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under Ar flow to obtain Co NPs embedded in $^{\mathrm{B,N}}$ co-doped carbon (denoted as D-Co/BNC) (Figs. $2(\mathrm{m})$ and $2(\mathrm{n})$ , and Figs. S19–S22 in the ESM). TEM images and corresponding particle size distribution show that D-Co/BNC possesses  smaller $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ nanoparticles  than ${\mathrm{Co/NC}},$ indicating  the critical role of B doping in preventing the aggregation of Co NPs (Fig. $2(\mathrm{n})_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ). The B dopants may act as anchors to establish strong interaction with the formed Co NPs in the thermal transformation process  to  resist  metal  aggregation  (see  Co  2p  XPS  results  as described  below).  However,  the  particle  size  of  Co  NPs  in  DCo/BNC $(24~\mathrm{nm})$ ) is still larger than that of ${\mathrm{Co/BNC}}.$ , suggesting that the flower-like morphology could spatially isolate metal ions in each layer to reduce the tendency of metal aggregation.
We  further  synthesized  a  hybrid  material  composed  of  ZIF
$8(Z{\bmod{,}}~\mathrm{Co})$ and  B/N-MOF-S  through  a  partial  transformation  of ZIF-8(Zn,  Co)  (Fig. S23  in  the  ESM).  After  calcination,  the resulted carbon composites exhibit different sizes of metal particles on  two  sides.  The  Co/BNC  side  shows  smaller  sizes  and  more homogeneous distribution of Co NPs than the Co/NC side (Fig. 2(p) and Fig. S24  in  the  ESM),  consistent  with  the  separate calcination results. Therefore, the proposed “spatial isolation and dopant anchoring” approach can build physical and/or chemical barriers  to  cooperatively  prevent  the  aggregation  of  metal  NPs during the high-temperature calcination of MOFs for the synthesis of carbon-supported metal NPs with both uniform dispersion and high loading.
Moreover,  the  different  doping  levels  of  carbon  supports  in $\mathsf{C o/B N C}$ and ${\mathrm{Co/NC}}$ result in distinct electronic states of Co due to  the  strong  metal–support  interaction.  As  can  be  seen  in  the high-resolution Co 2p XPS spectra, the line of $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ shows a shift to higher binding energy as compared with that of $\mathrm{Co/NC}$ (Fig. 2(l)).  Such  shifts  reflect  the  formation  of  electron-deficient Co  centers  in ${\mathrm{Co/BNC}},$ which  are  originated  from  strong coordination interaction between the BNC support and $\mathrm{Co}$ atoms in ${\mathrm{Co/BNC}}.$ The  modified  electronic  structure  of  Co  centers  in $\mathsf{C o/B N C}$ may contribute to the improvement of intrinsic activity in the catalytic reactions.
The proposed “spatial isolation and dopant anchoring” strategy can be easily extended to other ZIFs for the synthesis of supported high-density  metal  NPs.  ZIF- $8(\mathrm{Zn},\ \mathrm{\Ni})$ and  ZIF- $8(Z{\nmid},\mathrm{Cu})$ precursors both exhibit a rhombic dodecahedral morphology (Fig. S25 in the ESM). After boronic acid etching and calcination, the obtained  Ni/BNC  and $\mathrm{Cu/BNC}$ show  homogeneously  dispersed Ni  and $\mathrm{Cu}\ \mathrm{NPs}$ on  B,N  co-doped  carbon  supports,  respectively (Fig. S25 in the ESM).
Electrochemical  water  splitting  is  an  ecofriendly  pathway  for hydrogen  supply,  with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ produced  through  cathodic HER  and  anodic  oxygen  evolution  reaction  (OER),  respectively. The  OER  is  thermodynamically  unfavorable  and  also  requires high overpotentials to overcome the reaction energy barrier [35, 36]. Recently, HzOR assisted water electrolysis for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production has been proposed as a promising approach to replace the OER process  due  to  the  low  thermodynamic  oxidation  potential  of hydrazine $(-0.33~\mathrm{V}$ vs. reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE)) and the high safety when mixing gaseous products $\left(\Nu_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)$ in the cell [37–42]. To accelerate the advance of this relatively nascent realm, it is of vital importance to develop bifunctional catalysts with high performances for both HER and HzOR. However, there are very few catalysts that can simultaneously satisfy the HER and HzOR. The  characteristics  of $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ with  small  Co  particle  sizes, uniform dispersion, and high metal loadings have inspired us to investigate its catalytic performance for HER and HzOR.
The  electrocatalytic  activities  of  the  as-synthesized  samples toward  HER  were  first  investigated  in $1.0\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH  electrolyte using  a  typical  three-electrode  system.  The  linear  sweep voltammograms (LSV) tests of $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ exhibit small overpotentials of 105 and $225\mathrm{mV}$ to achieve 10 and $100\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, which are much lower than those of $\mathrm{Co/NC}$ (281 and $420~\mathrm{mV},$ ) and D-Co/BNC (205 and $325\mathrm{mV}$ ) (Figs. 3(a) and $3(\mathrm{b})$ ). Encouragingly,  the  overpotentials  of $\mathsf{C o/B N C}$ for  HER  are compared favorably to most of previously reported Co-contained electrocatalysts (Table S2 in the ESM). The Tafel plots of $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ show a slope of $113.9\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ , which is quite smaller than those of $\mathrm{Co/NC}$ $\mathrm{139.8\mV{\cdot}d e c^{-1}}$ )  and $\mathrm{D-Co/BNC}$ $(120.5\ \mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}})$ , suggesting the faster reaction kinetics of Co/BNC (Fig. 3(c)). It has been known that the current densities reflected by the LSV plots are determined by the number and intrinsic activity of the active sites.  The  number  of  accessible $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ sites  of  the  catalysts  was calculated based on the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -temperature programmed desorption experiment  and  the  electrochemical  active  surface  area  (ECSA) results  (Fig. 3(i),  and Fig. S26  and  Table  S1  in  the  ESM).  The results  indicate  that $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ possesses  the  largest  number  of accessible Co sites, which should be attributed to the smallest sizes of Co NPs in ${\mathrm{Co/BNC}}.$ . To further assess the intrinsic activity of active sites, the turnover frequency (TOF) was calculated based on the accessible $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ sites. $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ and D-Co/BNC possess similar TOF  values,  higher  than  that  of ${\mathrm{Co/NC}},$ which  implies  that  B doping may improve the intrinsic activity of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ sites (Fig. S27 in the ESM). Therefore, the superior activity of $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ should be attributed  to  its  high  number  of  exposed  active  sites  and  high intrinsic activity of Co center. The durability of $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ in HER was  further  investigated.  The  chronoamperometric  curve  of $\mathsf{C o/B N C}$ maintains a stable current density of $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at the overpotential  of $0.1~\mathrm{~V~}$ for  at  least $1,000~\mathrm{\min}$ (Fig. 3(d)). Meanwhile, the LSV curves of the used catalysts exhibit negligible decay  compared  with  the  initial  one  (the  inset  of Fig. 3(d)), suggesting the excellent stability of Co/BNC for HER.
The HzOR performance of the catalysts was then measured via a  three-electrode  system  using  a  carbon  cloth  supported electrocatalysts as the working electrode. The cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves of the catalysts show a rectangular shape in 1 M KOH solution.  In  the  presence  of ${\mathrm{N}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4},$ $\mathsf{C o/B N C}$ displays  the  lowest onset  potential  and  highest  current  density  among  the  catalysts from $-0.03\substack{-1.1\mathrm{~V~}}$ (vs. RHE, the same below if not mentioned), suggesting  the  highest  electrochemical  activity  of  Co/BNC  (Fig. S28 in the ESM). The anodic current density enhances with the hydrazine  concentration  from  0.1  to $0.4\mathrm{~M~}$ (Fig. S30(b)  in  the ESM).  The  carbon  cloth  substrate  exhibits  a  near-zero  HzOR current density, indicating the neglective activity of carbon cloth in HzOR (Fig. S28(d) in the ESM). An anodic peak at approximately $0.6\mathrm{V}$ was observed in the CV curve of $\mathsf{C o/B N C}$ . The peak current increases linearly with the square root of scan rate $(V^{\prime2})$ , indicating that this peak is originated from the hydrazine diffusion control processes (Fig. S29 in the ESM) [43]. As shown in the LSV curves, Co/BNC exhibits working potentials of 95 and $269\mathrm{mV}$ to achieve 10  and $100\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ,  respectively,  which  are  much  lower  than those  of $\mathrm{Co/NC}$ (278  and $724~\mathrm{mV})$ and  D-Co/BNC  (163  and $415\mathrm{mV},$ ), respectively (Figs. 3(e) and 3(f)). Co/BNC has the lowest Tafel  slope  value  among  the  catalysts  in $\mathrm{HzOR},$ implying  its advanced  HzOR  kinetics  (Fig. $3(\mathrm{g)}\rangle$ ).  Meanwhile,  the  LSV  curves normalized  by  the  accessible  sites  demonstrate  the  intrinsically superior catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ towards HzOR (Fig. S30(a) in  the  ESM).  Furthermore, $\mathrm{Co/BNC}$ possesses  good  stability  for HzOR with no decrease of current density for at least $600\ \mathrm{min}$ at the working potential of $0.2\mathrm{V}$ (Fig. $3(\mathrm{h})$ ).
To clarify the roles of B and N doping in boosting the catalytic performance of Co sites, the charge density difference and Bader charges  were  calculated.  The  results  suggest  that  the  electrondeficient state of Co sites and the electron accumulation on the O atoms of B–O species and N atoms (Fig. 4(a), and Figs. S39 and S40 in the ESM), indicating that the electron transfer from Co to the  heteroatom-doped  carbon  network,  which  is  also  consistent with the XPS results. This electron redistribution behavior on the metal–support interfaces may optimize the absorption energies of intermediates  and  thus  contribute  to  the  enhanced  intrinsic activity. These results suggest that the BNC support can establish strong  interaction  with  the  immobilized  Co  NPs  to  effectively stabilize the NPs and facilitate the formation of electron-deficient Co centers, and thus promote the hydrogen adsorption for HER and  dehydrogenation  process  for  HzOR  to  achieve  superior bifunctionality.
## 4    Conclusions
In  summary,  we  demonstrated  a “spatial  isolation  and  dopant anchoring” strategy  to  synergistically  prevent  metal  NPs  from aggregation  during  high-temperature  pyrolysis  of  MOFs.  We converted  a  bulk  MOF  into  dual-heteroatom-containing  flowerlike  MOF  sheets  to  spatially  isolate  metal  ions  and  increase  the number  of  anchoring  sites  in  the  MOF  precursor,  for  the generation of highly dispersed Co NPs on B,N co-doped carbon supports  with  high  loadings.  Co/BNC  is  demonstrated  to  be efficient  bifunctional  electrocatalysts  for  HER  and  HzOR. Experiment  results  and  DFT  calculations  reveal  that  the  high dispersion of Co NPs with a high number of accessible active sites and the strong interactions between $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ and B,N co-doped carbon support  can  modulate  the  electronic  structure  of  Co,  thereby promoting  the  thermodynamics  of  dehydrogenation  for  HzOR and hydrogen adsorption for HER. This work might open up a new  avenue  to  design  thermally  robust  and  highly  efficient nanocatalysts with controllable compositions and architectures for practical applications.
